📅 Day 16 – Encapsulation and Access Modifiers

Full Stack Web Development with Java Internship

Today’s session focused on the concepts of Encapsulation and Access Modifiers in Java. These concepts are essential for achieving data security and controlled access in Object-Oriented Programming.

🔹 Topics Covered
1️⃣ Encapsulation

Encapsulation is the process of binding data (variables) and methods together within a class and restricting direct access to the data.

The mentor explained:

Making data members private

Providing controlled access using getter and setter methods

Protecting data from unauthorized modification

Improving data security and maintainability

Key Points:

Data hiding is achieved using private variables.

Public getter methods → Used to retrieve data.

Public setter methods → Used to update data.

Ensures controlled access to class members.

2️⃣ Access Modifiers in Java

Access modifiers define the visibility and accessibility of classes, variables, and methods.

The following modifiers were discussed:

✅ public

Accessible from anywhere (within and outside package).

✅ private

Accessible only within the same class.

✅ protected

Accessible within the same package and in subclasses (even in different packages).

✅ default (no modifier)

Accessible only within the same package.

The session clarified how access modifiers help in implementing encapsulation effectively.

